# 13.7 Hybrid Retrieval (Graph + Vector Search)

---

# 13.7.1 Purpose

The Hybrid Retrieval Engine is responsible for retrieving the most relevant legal evidence for compliance analysis by combining semantic vector search with knowledge graph traversal.

Unlike traditional Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), which relies solely on vector similarity, LexisGraph performs GraphRAG by integrating semantic retrieval with structured legal relationships.

The output of the Hybrid Retrieval Engine is an evidence package that serves as the factual context for the LLM Reasoning Engine.

---

# 13.7.2 Objectives

The Hybrid Retrieval Engine shall:

✓ Perform semantic similarity search.

✓ Traverse legal relationships.

✓ Retrieve supporting definitions.

✓ Retrieve related obligations.

✓ Retrieve legal amendments.

✓ Retrieve penalties.

✓ Rank retrieved evidence.

✓ Remove duplicate results.

✓ Build explainable context for the LLM.

---

# 13.7.3 Hybrid Retrieval Architecture

```
                 Policy Clause

                       │

          ┌────────────┴────────────┐

          │                         │

     Vector Search             Graph Search

       (Qdrant)                  (Neo4j)

          │                         │

          └────────────┬────────────┘

                       │

              Hybrid Ranker

                       │

            Evidence Aggregator

                       │

           GraphRAG Context Builder

                       │

                  LLM Reasoning
```

Both retrieval mechanisms operate independently before their results are merged.

---

# 13.7.4 Retrieval Workflow

```
Policy Clause

↓

Generate Policy Embedding

↓

Metadata Filtering

↓

Vector Search

↓

Retrieve Top-K Clauses

↓

Graph Expansion

↓

Relationship Traversal

↓

Evidence Ranking

↓

Context Assembly

↓

LLM
```

---

# 13.7.5 Query Preparation

Each policy clause shall be converted into:

```
Embedding

Keywords

Named Entities

Organization Context

Legal Domain

Jurisdiction
```

This information is used throughout the retrieval process.

---

# 13.7.6 Metadata Filtering

Before semantic search, the engine shall filter candidates by:

```
Jurisdiction

Legal Domain

Language

Effective Date

Document Status

Knowledge Base Version

Organization Type

Industry
```

Filtering reduces irrelevant retrieval and improves latency.

---

# 13.7.7 Vector Search

Vector search shall retrieve semantically similar legal clauses.

Process

```
Policy Clause

↓

Embedding

↓

Qdrant Search

↓

Top-K Similar Clauses
```

Default retrieval size

```
Top 20
```

The retrieval size shall remain configurable.

---

# 13.7.8 Graph Expansion

Each retrieved clause shall become a graph traversal entry point.

The retrieval engine shall expand to connected nodes including:

```
Definitions

Requirements

Obligations

Exceptions

Authorities

Penalties

Cross References

Amendments

Related Clauses

Schedules
```

Graph expansion shall be depth-limited to prevent excessive traversal.

---

# 13.7.9 Graph Traversal Strategy

Recommended traversal depth

```
1–3 hops
```

Traversal shall prioritize:

```
REQUIRES

DEFINES

REFERENCES

AMENDS

SUPERSEDES

PENALIZED_BY

APPLIES_TO

RELATED_TO
```

Traversal depth and relationship priorities shall be configurable.

---

# 13.7.10 Candidate Merging

The retrieval engine shall combine:

```
Vector Results

+

Graph Results

↓

Merged Candidate Set
```

Duplicate clauses shall be removed before ranking.

---

# 13.7.11 Hybrid Ranking

Each candidate shall receive a composite relevance score.

Ranking factors may include:

```
Semantic Similarity

Graph Distance

Relationship Importance

Document Authority

Effective Date

Jurisdiction Match

Organization Context

Knowledge Base Version
```

The weighting strategy shall be configurable and periodically evaluated using benchmark datasets.

---

# 13.7.12 Result Deduplication

Duplicate evidence shall be removed using:

```
Clause ID

Document ID

Content Similarity

Reference Mapping
```

Only unique evidence shall be passed to the Context Builder.

---

# 13.7.13 Context Window Optimization

The engine shall optimize context size by:

Removing duplicate clauses

Removing redundant definitions

Grouping related clauses

Prioritizing high-confidence evidence

Keeping the prompt within model token limits

Context optimization shall preserve legal meaning.

---

# 13.7.14 Evidence Package

The GraphRAG Context Builder shall produce a structured evidence package.

Example

```json
{
  "policy_clause": "...",
  "retrieved_clauses": [],
  "definitions": [],
  "obligations": [],
  "exceptions": [],
  "penalties": [],
  "references": [],
  "metadata": {}
}
```

The evidence package shall be the only legal context supplied to the LLM.

---

# 13.7.15 Explainability Support

Each retrieved item shall include:

```
Clause ID

Document

Retrieval Method

Similarity Score

Graph Distance

Relationship Type

Reason for Retrieval
```

This information shall be available for report generation and auditing.

---

# 13.7.16 Retrieval Cache

Frequently executed retrievals may be cached.

Cache keys may include:

```
Embedding Hash

Jurisdiction

Knowledge Base Version

Organization Type
```

Cache invalidation shall occur when the Knowledge Base version changes.

---

# 13.7.17 Performance Goals

Vector retrieval

```
≤ 500 ms
```

Graph traversal

```
≤ 500 ms
```

Hybrid ranking

```
≤ 200 ms
```

Complete evidence package

```
≤ 1 second
```

Performance targets are dependent on infrastructure and dataset size.

---

# 13.7.18 Monitoring

The system shall monitor:

```
Average Retrieval Latency

Top-K Retrieval Accuracy

Graph Traversal Depth

Cache Hit Rate

Retrieved Clause Count

Ranking Duration

Duplicate Removal Rate
```

These metrics shall be available through the Administration Dashboard.

---

# 13.7.19 Security

The retrieval engine shall:

Restrict access to authorized backend services

Prevent arbitrary graph queries from end users

Validate metadata filters

Protect against excessive traversal depth

Log retrieval operations for auditing

Never expose internal graph identifiers directly to clients

---

# 13.7.20 Future Enhancements

The architecture shall support:

Cross-encoder reranking

Hybrid dense + sparse retrieval

Personalized retrieval based on organization profile

Temporal retrieval for historical compliance analysis

Citation-aware ranking

Learning-to-rank models

Multi-jurisdiction evidence fusion

---

# 13.7.21 Acceptance Criteria

The Hybrid Retrieval Engine shall be considered complete when:

✓ Semantic vector search retrieves relevant clauses.

✓ Graph traversal expands related legal knowledge.

✓ Metadata filtering reduces irrelevant results.

✓ Hybrid ranking merges and prioritizes evidence.

✓ Duplicate results are removed.

✓ A structured evidence package is generated.

✓ Retrieval metadata supports explainability.

✓ Performance targets are achieved under expected workloads.

---

# 13.7.22 Summary

The Hybrid Retrieval Engine combines semantic similarity search with relationship-aware graph traversal to deliver high-quality legal evidence for compliance analysis. By integrating Qdrant's vector retrieval with Neo4j's knowledge graph, the engine produces an explainable, ranked, and structured evidence package that enables the LLM to reason over authoritative legal knowledge rather than relying on its internal memory. This hybrid approach forms the foundation of LexisGraph's GraphRAG architecture and significantly improves retrieval precision, transparency, and trustworthiness.

